#
# A change to this file on claude/awesome-rubin-2godw2 starts the opt-in
# workflow .github/workflows/takx-desktop-prototype.yaml (ci/pipeline.py plan
# reads it). Lines starting with '#' are comments; the settings are
# 'arches=<comma separated list of amd64, arm64>' (default: both) and
# 'providers=build|cached' (default: build; cached skips the provider waves).
#
# run 1: first build on hosted arm64 runners, with the amd64 control.
# run 2: inputs fetched once per architecture and stored (Maven Central answered
#        the per-job fetch with HTTP 429); ELF machine audit of the outputs.
# run 3: Kernel and client from the provider cache only (providers=cached); the
#        logs of the Kernel controls are uploaded, because the ones that load
#        the native engine failed on amd64 in run 2; arm64 client lock tracked.
# run 4: amd64 only, from the cache, with diagnostics for the amd64 Kernel
#        control failure "real GDAL JNI unavailable" (ci/ARCH-CACHE-NOTE.md).
# run 5: run 4 never reached the Kernel: its inputs key was new (a JSON file
#        was added below takx/), so it fetched everything again and Maven
#        Central answered HTTP 429. A fetch now starts from the stored inputs
#        (ci/inputs.sh seed) and asks the Maven Central mirror after a 429.
#        Both architectures from the provider cache; ci/diagnose-controls.sh
#        records why a Kernel control fails.
# run 6: arm64 only, from the provider cache. The assembler is amd64-only and
#        belongs to another part of the prototype, so the arm64 tree copy now
#        carries overrides/arm64/prototype/assemble.patch (JogAmp aarch64
#        natives, arm64 Kernel JAR name); the tracked assembler is unchanged.
#        First arm64 run that can get past "assemble: missing input".
# run 7: both architectures from the provider cache. Run 5 found why six
#        Kernel controls failed on amd64: the MD5 of that Kernel JAR starts
#        with 0, the stock loader drops that zero from its directory name and
#        kernel/controls.py did not (fixed). The client lock is now compared
#        per build lineage (client/inputs.lock.<arch>.ci.json), so the amd64
#        leg can go on to assembly and boot.
# run 8: the same, with the boot group waiting before 'boot.sh check' (it says
#        ok only for a JVM that has been up for more than 120 seconds) and the
#        frame of the attempt kept as an artifact. Replaces run 7 while that
#        was still queued.
# run 9: repeat of run 8, which was the first run with every job green on
#        both architectures. Adds the ELF machine record of the assembled
#        tree and the tree receipt under a name the artifact upload keeps.
# run 10: the prototype patch of the assembler may now match at other line
#        numbers (the tracked assembler is being changed); otherwise as run 9.
# run 11: the packages this workflow writes turned out to be readable without
#        a login, and the stored inputs held the bytedeco FFmpeg native JAR
#        ("nonfree and unredistributable"). ci/inputs.sh save now leaves the
#        files of ci/inputs-not-stored.json out, the key prefix is 'inputs 2'
#        (so both architectures fetch again, seeded, and store a new object),
#        and the client job downloads the JAR from Maven Central and checks its
#        pin. Also: an arm64 assembly or boot failure fails the leg unless the
#        assembler patch was skipped. From the provider cache.
# run 12: the whole workflow as a first-time user starts it (providers=build),
#        with every provider already in the cache: the three provider waves
#        have to restore and build nothing. ci/inputs-seeds.json now names the
#        objects run 11 stored without the files of ci/inputs-not-stored.json.
arches=amd64,arm64
providers=build
